    "toolSubheading": "Answer a few questions about your property to create your custom preparation checklist.",
    "howItWorks": "The home selling checklist groups your sale into seven distinct stages. Stage one starts with planning the sale. Stage two focuses on repairs and physical preparation to address safety concerns before buyers tour. Stage three covers disclosures and legal documentation required by your state. Stage four covers listing and showings. Stage five handles offers and contract negotiation. Stage six covers being under contract, and stage seven manages closing and move-out.\n\nBefore spending money on preparation, calculate your estimated proceeds and evaluate potential tax obligations. Closing expenses reduce your cash from the sale, so calculate your net figures using the [closing cost calculator](/mortgage/closing-cost-calculator/). Under [IRS Publication 523](https://www.irs.gov/publications/p523), single sellers can exclude up to $250,000 of gain on a primary residence. Married couples filing jointly can exclude up to $500,000 of gain. To qualify for this exclusion, you must have owned and occupied the house as your primary home for two of the five years preceding the sale. If the home was used as an investment property, review [IRS Publication 527](https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527) and calculate taxes using the [capital gains calculator](/real-estate/capital-gains-calculator/).\n\nFor repairs and preparation, focus your budget on functional defects and safety issues that an inspector will note. Get quotes before starting any large cosmetic remodel. Buyers often price condition into their purchase offers. You can also consider a pre-listing inspection. If your home uses private waste treatment, the [EPA septic guidance](https://www.epa.gov/septic) notes that septic tanks should be inspected regularly by a professional and pumped as needed.\n\nDisclosures form the legal foundation of your transaction. State law sets required property disclosure forms, which vary by state. If the house was built before 1978, federal law requires sellers to disclose known lead-based paint and provide the official pamphlet per the [EPA real estate disclosure rules](https://www.epa.gov/lead/real-estate-disclosure). Buyers also receive a 10-day window to conduct an independent lead inspection unless they waive it. If the property belongs to a homeowners association, request resale documents early because they take time.\n\nYour selling path shapes the rest of your preparation. Selling by owner adds planning, showing-safety, and contract-review steps. For rental sales, [HUD tenant rights resources](https://www.hud.gov/topics/rental_assistance/tenantrights) link to state laws that govern required entry notices and security deposit return rules. The climate switches add three prep items. A vacant house in a freezing climate gets a keep-the-heat-on or winterize step, a wildfire area adds clearing dead vegetation near the house, and a hurricane coast adds a plan for securing the house during hurricane season.\n\nStart your sale preparation by calculating net proceeds and completing safety repairs, then assemble disclosures before marketing the home. If you are coordinating a purchase alongside your sale, follow our [guide to buying and selling a house at the same time](/guides/how-to-buy-and-sell-a-house-at-the-same-time/). Once an offer is accepted, the buyer will run their own [final walkthrough checklist](/checklists/final-walkthrough-checklist/), so leave every included item in place and finish agreed repairs before that visit. Complete the questionnaire above, click the \"Print or save as PDF\" button, and begin working through your customized tasks.",
