    "howItWorks": "Our rental checklist organizes preparation into six distinct chronological sections. Section one covers steps before you apply: setting your rent limit, confirming the listing is real, checking the landlord or management company, and the flood map. Section two provides an apartment tour checklist to inspect physical conditions like water, active signs of pests, noise, and safety. Section three lists questions to ask the landlord about utilities and maintenance responsibilities. Section four lists the lease clauses to read, while section five guides you through the application and tenant-screening disclosures. Section six handles move-in day, linking directly to our [move-in condition checklist](/checklists/move-in-move-out-checklist/) so you can document baseline damage.\n\nOn the physical tour, test the home from the inside out to confirm everything works. Check the unit itself for overall condition, pests, water, noise, and safety. The [National Fire Protection Association](https://www.nfpa.org/education-and-research/home-fire-safety/smoke-alarms) advises smoke alarms inside every bedroom, outside each sleeping area, and on every level of the home. NFPA advises testing them monthly and replacing each alarm every 10 years.\n\nBefore you pay an application fee, ask direct questions about routine maintenance and study the proposed lease contract. Ask questions about repair responsibilities, package handling, and parking. Review the written agreement for fees, repair obligations, and break-lease terms. State laws govern security-deposit caps, return deadlines, and landlord entry-notice requirements rather than a single federal standard. You can verify your specific local protections on the [HUD tenant rights portal](https://www.hud.gov/topics/rental_assistance/tenantrights). If you are weighing whether leasing fits your finances long-term, compare ownership costs with our [renting vs buying guide](/compare/renting-vs-buying/).\n\nWhat you check changes based on whether you rent an apartment, a whole house, or a single room. A house inspection checklist demands attention to yard, utility, and maintenance duties. You must identify who pays for lawn care, snow, pests, and filters. An apartment focuses on management-company reviews, building noise, trash, and package handling. If this is your first time setting up a lease, review our [first apartment checklist](/checklists/first-apartment-checklist/).\n\nYour geographic location also shifts what matters on a property walkthrough. With Winters reach freezing on, a house or small-landlord rental adds a question about the required winter heat setting and who pays if a pipe freezes, and move-in adds finding the water shutoff. On a hurricane-prone coast, the list adds asking who puts up storm shutters and, at move-in, knowing your evacuation zone. Check the [FEMA Flood Map Service Center](https://msc.fema.gov/portal/home) to see if the property sits in a high-risk flood area, since standard renters policies generally do not cover flood damage. At the same time, watch for deceptive listings across all regions. The [FTC rental scam guide](https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/rental-listing-scams) warns renters to walk away if an advertiser pressures you to pay before visiting, demands wire transfers or cryptocurrency, or offers rent far below local rates.\n\nTake a fresh printed copy on every tour and get every verbal promise written into the lease. State and city law sets deposit limits and entry notice, so check your state on the [HUD tenant rights page](https://www.hud.gov/topics/rental_assistance/tenantrights) before you pay anything.",
